  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Turf cutters keep digging butter out of Irish bogs. In 2011 two men near Tullamore hit a carved wooden vessel seven feet down.",
        "visual_query": "irish peat bog turf cutting",
        "visual_fallback": "peat bog"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Inside sat about fifty kilos of pale, waxy bog butter. It still carried a faint dairy smell.",
        "visual_query": "wooden keg ancient carved vessel",
        "visual_fallback": "wooden barrel"
      },
      {
        "narration": "This is not a one-off. Ireland has been burying butter in kegs, churns and barrels since the first century AD. But why bury food?",
        "visual_query": "irish bogland misty open landscape",
        "visual_fallback": "misty moorland"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Bogs are cold, acidic and starved of oxygen, essentially a working fridge. Bristol researchers showed in 2003 that some finds are dairy, others animal fat.",
        "visual_query": "dark peat soil cut bank",
        "visual_fallback": "dark soil"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Butter was a luxury in early medieval Ireland, so some stashes were probably hidden from raiders. Others were simply left behind, and never collected.",
        "visual_query": "irish countryside stone ruins field",
        "visual_fallback": "irish countryside"
      }
    ],
